  you already think in components. Costs: a build step, a dependency tree, and more framework concepts than the app has
  actual logic — for four fields and a timer, the scaffolding would outweigh the program. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mI recommend B.[0m The interesting, bug-prone part of this app is the reminder logic — clock handling, what happens across
  sleep, not firing the same reminder twice. B is the cheapest approach that lets me test that logic directly, in
  isolation, without a browser or a build. A can't test it; C tests it fine but makes you pay for a toolchain to get
  there. B is also trivially upgradeable to C later — the modules become the logic layer and only [38;5;153mui.js[39m gets replaced.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which structure should we build on? → B: vanilla ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the tooling question — cheapest to answer before any code exists, and whatever you pick lands in the spec so every
  later task inherits it.

  My recommendation: [1mlinting/formatting and unit tests, skip the rest.[0m ESLint + Prettier is the stack standard and costs
  one setup task. Vitest earns its place immediately because the reminder logic is the part worth testing. End-to-end
  (Playwright) is the only way to actually exercise the notification permission prompt and the backgrounded-tab path —
  genuinely useful here, but it roughly doubles the setup and is slow to run; I'd add it later if the reminder behavior
  turns out flaky in real use. Fuzz/mutation testing has nothing meaningful to chew on in an app this size.
